EduTrack Project – Daily Learning Report

Day 1 – 9 October 2026

Topic: Starting the EduTrack Project

On the first day, I started working on my EduTrack project. I learned how to set up a Node.js project and run it using the terminal. I also started designing the homepage using HTML and CSS. I worked on the navigation bar, logo, and login buttons for teachers and students. This helped me understand how to create the basic structure and design of a website.

Day 2 – 12 October 2026

Topic: Learning EJS and Database Connectivity

On the second day, I learned how to use EJS with Express.js to create dynamic web pages. I explored how forms send data to the backend and how MongoDB can store that information. I also started understanding how teachers and students would log in and access their respective dashboards.

Day 3 – 17 October 2026

Topic: Teacher and Student Registration

On this day, I worked on the registration pages for teachers and students. I used EJS and CSS to design the forms and added fields for collecting user information. I also learned how to organize EJS files inside the views folder. While working, I faced some page-rendering errors and learned how to fix them by checking the routes and file paths.

Day 4 – 18 October 2026

Topic: Database and Dashboard Design

On the fourth day, I focused on the database structure and dashboard design. I learned how to organize teacher and student information using MongoDB and Mongoose. I also worked on planning separate dashboards for teachers and students. The teacher dashboard would allow teachers to manage student records and add important topics, while the student dashboard would help students view important study topics and their academic information.

Day 5 – 19 October 2026

Topic: Backend Development and Login System

On the fifth day, I worked on connecting the frontend with the backend using Node.js and Express.js. I learned how to handle registration and login requests and how to use MongoDB models to manage data. I also explored bcrypt for password security and express-session for maintaining login sessions.

I worked on the teacher dashboard, where teachers can add and delete important topics and manage student records. I also learned about the student approval system, in which a student's roll number must be authorized before registration. During development, I faced some errors related to EJS rendering, database operations, and registration. By checking the code and server messages, I learned how to identify and solve these problems.